    "# Quantum Multi-Objective Portfolio Optimization: D-Wave Pareto Frontier Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook explores **quantum multi-objective portfolio optimization** using D-Wave systems for Pareto frontier analysis. Building on the classical baseline established in `pareto_optimization_classical.ipynb`, this notebook investigates where quantum computing might show genuine advantages.\n",
    "\n",
    "## **Objectives:**\n",
    "1. **Maximize Expected Return** - Portfolio profitability\n",
    "2. **Minimize Risk (Volatility)** - Portfolio stability  \n",
    "3. **Maximize Liquidity** - Ease of trading\n",
    "\n",
    "## **Quantum Methods Investigated:**\n",
    "- **D-Wave Classical Multi-Objective** (BQM with multiple objectives)\n",
    "- **D-Wave Quantum Multi-Objective** (CQM Pareto exploration)\n",
    "- **Hybrid Quantum-Classical** (Combined approaches)\n",
    "\n",
    "## **Key Research Questions:**\n",
    "1. Can quantum methods find more complete Pareto frontiers than classical approaches?\n",
    "2. Do quantum solutions show better diversity across objectives?\n",
    "3. How does quantum performance compare to classical baseline?\n",
    "4. What are the computational trade-offs of quantum approaches?\n",
    "5. Where do quantum methods show genuine advantages?\n",
    "\n",
    "## **Comparison Framework:**\n",
    "This notebook provides a **fair quantum vs classical comparison** by:\n",
    "- Using identical problem formulations (Return-Risk-Liquidity optimization)\n",
    "- Applying same evaluation metrics (Pareto frontier coverage, diversity, quality)\n",
    "- Testing on same datasets (Full 439 assets, Wide 680+ assets)\n",
    "- Focusing on multi-objective problems where quantum could excel\n",
    "\n",
    "**⚠️ D-Wave Machine Time Required**: This notebook requires access to D-Wave quantum systems and will consume quantum processing units (QPUs). Ensure you have adequate D-Wave credits before running."
   ]
  },
